1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,375
Bhai pressed the button to pay the bill,

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:04,411
and the debt still went up.

3
00:00:04,411 --> 00:00:07,126
The button was called minimum due. He thought,

4
00:00:07,126 --> 00:00:08,822
that's today's work done.

5
00:00:08,822 --> 00:00:11,876
In truth, that very night a meter started running.

6
00:00:11,876 --> 00:00:14,930
That meter kept running month after month, completely silently.

7
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:19,516
Late at night, Bhai is looking at his phone in bed.

8
00:00:19,516 --> 00:00:24,611
The card app is open, with the bill in red at the top.

9
00:00:24,611 --> 00:00:28,687
Below are the buttons, total due and minimum due.

10
00:00:28,687 --> 00:00:32,763
The minimum due amount looks very small.

11
00:00:32,763 --> 00:00:35,820
Bhai smiles and presses that one.

12
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:38,887
A few months later Behen looks at his statement.

13
00:00:38,887 --> 00:00:41,571
He hasn't made any new purchase at all.

14
00:00:41,571 --> 00:00:45,406
Still the bill is bigger than before. Behen asks,

15
00:00:45,406 --> 00:00:49,623
you did pay, didn't you? Bhai says, yes, every month,

16
00:00:49,623 --> 00:00:50,390
on time.

17
00:00:50,390 --> 00:00:52,522
So some questions come up here.

18
00:00:52,522 --> 00:00:55,720
What actually happens when you pay only the minimum due?

19
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:58,208
Why does the bill not finish for years?

20
00:00:58,208 --> 00:01:01,762
And why is card interest so much heavier than other loans?

21
00:01:01,762 --> 00:01:04,960
Today we'll understand these with a story, in simple language.

22
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,869
And at the end we'll also see the way out.

23
00:01:07,869 --> 00:01:10,455
Because this trap is not only Bhai's.

24
00:01:10,455 --> 00:01:13,041
It's the festive sale season, phone, TV, fridge,

25
00:01:13,041 --> 00:01:14,981
everything is going on the card.

26
00:01:14,981 --> 00:01:17,890
So this story may be from your home too.

27
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:21,585
Let's start from the beginning. A festive sale was on.

28
00:01:21,585 --> 00:01:24,541
Bhai's old phone had become slow.

29
00:01:24,541 --> 00:01:28,236
He saw a new phone online, a big discount, plus a card offer.

30
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:32,300
He thought, let me buy it now, I'll pay later.

31
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:34,112
Up to here everything was fine.

32
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:37,010
A credit card is actually a loan from the bank.

33
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:40,633
You spend today, the bank pays the shopkeeper.

34
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:43,532
At the end of the month the bank sends a statement.

35
00:01:43,532 --> 00:01:46,430
A statement means the account of the whole month's spending.

36
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:48,053
Now understand one good thing.

37
00:01:48,053 --> 00:01:51,624
If you pay the full bill on time, there is no interest.

38
00:01:51,624 --> 00:01:54,869
This is called the interest-free period, meaning a time of free credit.

39
00:01:54,869 --> 00:01:58,440
Card companies give this period of 20 to 50 days.

40
00:01:58,440 --> 00:01:58,793
Think,

41
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:02,672
The bank is giving you free money for about 50 days.

42
00:02:02,672 --> 00:02:05,494
No interest, no fees. There is just one condition:

43
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:07,610
you must pay the full bill on time.

44
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:11,490
That condition is the key to the whole game, remember it.

45
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:14,963
Then the statement came. The bill was as much as the price of the phone.

46
00:02:14,963 --> 00:02:17,278
Salary was still a while away,

47
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:19,978
and the house rent had to be paid too.

48
00:02:19,978 --> 00:02:23,065
The app showed an easy way out: minimum due.

49
00:02:23,065 --> 00:02:25,380
Just a small part of the bill.

50
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:27,459
Minimum due means that small amount,

51
00:02:27,459 --> 00:02:30,232
which, if you pay it, the bank won't call you a defaulter.

52
00:02:30,232 --> 00:02:33,698
No late fee will be charged, and your account keeps running. Bhai felt,

53
00:02:33,698 --> 00:02:37,510
the bank itself is giving this option. So it must be safe.

54
00:02:37,510 --> 00:02:40,744
And the logic makes sense too. Papa always used to say,

55
00:02:40,744 --> 00:02:43,978
pay your installments on time, your record will stay good. Bhai thought,

56
00:02:43,978 --> 00:02:46,566
I am paying the minimum on time too.

57
00:02:46,566 --> 00:02:49,800
I'll pay the rest next month. What's wrong with that?

58
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,720
What's wrong is what the app doesn't write in big letters.

59
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:56,289
The moment the full bill isn't paid, the free credit period ends.

60
00:02:56,289 --> 00:02:59,859
Interest is charged not just on the remaining amount, but on the whole bill.

61
00:02:59,859 --> 00:03:02,130
And often from the very day of purchase.

62
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:07,300
So what Bhai thought turned out the opposite. He had thought,

63
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,120
a little interest would be charged on the remaining amount.

64
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:14,820
In reality, you don't get a free period even on new spending.

65
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:17,640
Until the old bill is cleared,

66
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:20,460
every new swipe attracts interest right away.

67
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:23,663
Now the next question. Why is card interest so heavy?

68
00:03:23,663 --> 00:03:26,866
For that, let's first understand Papa's PPF way of thinking.

69
00:03:26,866 --> 00:03:28,468
Because that is our normal,

70
00:03:28,468 --> 00:03:31,030
the yardstick we measure every interest rate with.

71
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:35,677
Papa's cupboard has a PPF passbook.

72
00:03:35,677 --> 00:03:41,485
PPF gives about 7.1 percent interest a year.

73
00:03:41,485 --> 00:03:46,713
Papa considers this a good return. Now think the opposite.

74
00:03:46,713 --> 00:03:51,360
On a card, you don't earn interest, you pay it.

75
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,239
And card interest is many times the PPF interest.

76
00:03:55,239 --> 00:03:58,471
In the statement, it is written per month.

77
00:03:58,471 --> 00:04:01,704
A small percent per month sounds light.

78
00:04:01,704 --> 00:04:04,290
Add it up over a year and your eyes open.

79
00:04:04,290 --> 00:04:06,812
On top of that, this interest is compounding.

80
00:04:06,812 --> 00:04:10,774
Compounding means interest on interest. The interest charged this month,

81
00:04:10,774 --> 00:04:13,296
It got added to next month's bill.

82
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:16,178
Next month, interest will be charged on that bigger bill.

83
00:04:16,178 --> 00:04:18,700
The bill swells up a little every month.

84
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:23,147
Think of debt as a bucket that you want to empty.

85
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:26,381
Every month you take out one mugful of water.

86
00:04:26,381 --> 00:04:30,827
But the tap is running from above, the interest tap. However much you take out,

87
00:04:30,827 --> 00:04:34,870
that much comes right back. The bucket never gets empty.

88
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:37,234
And the story isn't over yet.

89
00:04:37,234 --> 00:04:40,779
The government also charges GST on interest and fees.

90
00:04:40,779 --> 00:04:43,537
GST means the tax charged on services.

91
00:04:43,537 --> 00:04:47,476
On card interest and fees, it is 18 percent.

92
00:04:47,476 --> 00:04:49,840
So there's tax even on top of the interest.

93
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:54,606
Now read the lines on Bhai's statement. Finance charges, meaning interest.

94
00:04:54,606 --> 00:04:58,505
Below that, GST. Sometimes a late fee, and then GST on that too.

95
00:04:58,505 --> 00:05:03,271
Bhai thought he was paying off the price of the phone.

96
00:05:03,271 --> 00:05:07,170
In reality, he was paying interest and tax.

97
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:09,583
Then one month his salary came late.

98
00:05:09,583 --> 00:05:13,030
He missed the minimum due too. Now a late fee was charged,

99
00:05:13,030 --> 00:05:16,477
GST was charged on it, and interest was already running anyway.

100
00:05:16,477 --> 00:05:19,580
A little delay made the bill even bigger.

101
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:21,729
And one thing happens quietly.

102
00:05:21,729 --> 00:05:25,311
Late payment gets recorded in your credit score.

103
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:28,176
Credit score means your report card for borrowing.

104
00:05:28,176 --> 00:05:31,399
If you want to take a home loan or car loan tomorrow,

105
00:05:31,399 --> 00:05:33,190
the bank looks at exactly this.

106
00:05:33,190 --> 00:05:37,127
Now the biggest question. Bhai is paying money every month.

107
00:05:37,127 --> 00:05:39,632
Then why does the bill never finish?

108
00:05:39,632 --> 00:05:42,137
The answer is hidden in this,

109
00:05:42,137 --> 00:05:45,000
where the money you pay actually goes.

110
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,056
When you pay the minimum due,

111
00:05:47,056 --> 00:05:50,483
the bank first cuts its own share. First tax, then fees,

112
00:05:50,483 --> 00:05:52,196
then interest. Whatever is left,

113
00:05:52,196 --> 00:05:54,595
only that reduces the actual debt.

114
00:05:54,595 --> 00:05:56,651
The actual debt is called the principal,

115
00:05:56,651 --> 00:05:59,050
meaning what you really spent.

116
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:01,893
The minimum due is small, and the interest part is big.

117
00:06:01,893 --> 00:06:05,051
So the principal only changes a little each month.

118
00:06:05,051 --> 00:06:07,262
It's like you're running on a treadmill.

119
00:06:07,262 --> 00:06:10,420
You're sweating fully, but you stay in the same place.

120
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:13,366
Some banks also tell you this in the statement,

121
00:06:13,366 --> 00:06:16,681
If you only pay the minimum, when will the bill ever end?

122
00:06:16,681 --> 00:06:19,995
Bhai read that line carefully for the first time.

123
00:06:19,995 --> 00:06:23,310
The answer wasn't in months. The answer was in years.

124
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:27,028
Think about it, a phone that will become old in a few years.

125
00:06:27,028 --> 00:06:31,119
The debt may last even longer than that. A new phone will come,

126
00:06:31,119 --> 00:06:34,837
and the installments of the old phone will still be running.

127
00:06:34,837 --> 00:06:37,440
This is the real trap of the minimum due.

128
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:41,879
And in the meantime, life doesn't stop. Next month Mummy's medicine,

129
00:06:41,879 --> 00:06:44,300
school fees, a wedding gift.

130
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:46,721
A little more went on the card.

131
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:49,545
A new bill piled on top of the old bill.

132
00:06:49,545 --> 00:06:52,370
Now even the minimum due started looking bigger.

133
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:54,892
Mummy set down the tea and asked, beta,

134
00:06:54,892 --> 00:06:57,414
where is the money going every month?

135
00:06:57,414 --> 00:07:00,656
Bhai had no answer. The salary came,

136
00:07:00,656 --> 00:07:03,898
and in the first week it went into the card.

137
00:07:03,898 --> 00:07:06,060
The savings slowly ran out.

138
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:07,709
This is the biggest cost.

139
00:07:07,709 --> 00:07:10,017
The interest isn't only going to the bank.

140
00:07:10,017 --> 00:07:11,995
It is eating your savings.

141
00:07:11,995 --> 00:07:14,303
The money that could have gone into a SIP,

142
00:07:14,303 --> 00:07:15,952
could have become an emergency fund,

143
00:07:15,952 --> 00:07:18,590
is flying away in interest every month.

144
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:21,843
So where did the mistake happen? The mistake wasn't buying the phone.

145
00:07:21,843 --> 00:07:24,734
The mistake was thinking the minimum due is paying the bill.

146
00:07:24,734 --> 00:07:26,903
The minimum due is not paying the bill.

147
00:07:26,903 --> 00:07:29,071
It only tells the bank,

148
00:07:29,071 --> 00:07:31,240
that I haven't run away yet.

149
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:34,691
Now how do you get out? The simplest way, pay the full bill,

150
00:07:34,691 --> 00:07:36,762
every time. Use the card only as much as

151
00:07:36,762 --> 00:07:39,178
you can pay off by the end of the month.

152
00:07:39,178 --> 00:07:44,010
Then that interest-free credit of up to 50 days really works for you.

153
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:47,342
If the bill is big and you can't pay it all at once,

154
00:07:47,342 --> 00:07:50,342
then convert it into EMI. Talk to the bank,

155
00:07:50,342 --> 00:07:54,007
or look for the option in the app. EMI means a fixed installment, for a fixed time.

156
00:07:54,007 --> 00:07:57,340
Interest is often lower, and you know in advance when it will end.

157
00:07:57,340 --> 00:08:00,443
There is one more way, balance transfer.

158
00:08:00,443 --> 00:08:05,098
That means shifting the old bill to another card,

159
00:08:05,098 --> 00:08:08,201
where the interest is lower for some time.

160
00:08:08,201 --> 00:08:11,821
Read its fees and terms carefully.

161
00:08:11,821 --> 00:08:17,510
Otherwise you'll escape one trap only to land in another.

162
00:08:17,510 --> 00:08:21,556
Or take a personal loan and pay off the full card bill in one go.

163
00:08:21,556 --> 00:08:24,046
The interest on a personal loan isn't low either,

164
00:08:24,046 --> 00:08:26,225
but it is often lighter than the card.

165
00:08:26,225 --> 00:08:29,960
And after the loan, don't start filling up the card again.

166
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:32,752
One small habit also helps.

167
00:08:32,752 --> 00:08:36,242
Set the card's auto-debit on the full bill, not the minimum.

168
00:08:36,242 --> 00:08:39,731
My sister does exactly this. For her, the card is a convenience, not a debt.

169
00:08:39,731 --> 00:08:43,570
This is not investment advice, just plain simple math.

170
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:47,373
Here's the moral of the story. Minimum due is a convenience,

171
00:08:47,373 --> 00:08:50,795
not a way out. It saves you from today's trouble,

172
00:08:50,795 --> 00:08:53,837
but makes tomorrow's bill bigger.

173
00:08:53,837 --> 00:08:58,020
The day paying the full bill feels hard, change your path, not the button.

174
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:00,763
In the next video we'll open up another trap,

175
00:09:00,763 --> 00:09:04,289
one that shows up everywhere this festive season.

176
00:09:04,289 --> 00:09:08,600
No cost EMI. Is it really no cost?

177
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:12,910
Until then, read your card statement carefully, especially the small print.

178
00:09:12,910 --> 00:09:15,944
Now you tell me, answer directly in the comments.

179
00:09:15,944 --> 00:09:20,278
Have you ever paid only the minimum due? Yes or no,

180
00:09:20,278 --> 00:09:24,613
just write that. If you know someone's story like this,

181
00:09:24,613 --> 00:09:28,080
write that too, it will help others.
